From fbcefad72248d57d1eb83ee02b97e2b613035c20 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 17:17:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 097/206] feat(shorebird): document --shorebird in
 analyze_snapshot's help

Their analyze_snapshot documents seven flags. Ours documented three, and the
one Shorebird flag we do implement -- --shorebird, present since B2 -- was not
among them. A flag that works and is undocumented is only slightly better than
one that does not exist.

The three lines added are byte-identical to theirs, padding included. Their
separator before "Shorebird options:" is a bare newline while every other line
is padded to 76 columns; that was measured, not guessed, and the check compares
trailing whitespace precisely so it cannot be got wrong quietly.

The six flags we do not implement are deliberately still absent. A help text
that advertises a flag the parser rejects is worse than one that is short. They
are listed in a comment at the point where theirs prints them, with what each
would actually need:

- --dump_object_pool_link_data and --no_pp_hash need op_subgraph_hash, a
  private subsystem this tree does not have. ObjectPoolLinkInfo::WriteToStream
  is already implemented and byte-verified against their files, so what is
  missing is the producer, not the format.
- --dump_blobs needs the four snapshot region sizes, which
  Dart_SnapshotAnalyzerInformation does not carry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc b/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
index 077fc53665d..adf4a13fe0f 100644
--- a/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
+++ b/runtime/bin/analyze_snapshot.cc
@@ -51,6 +51,31 @@ static void PrintUsage() {
 "  Print the SDK version.                                                    \n"
 "--out                                                                       \n"
 "  Path to generate the analysis results JSON.                               \n"
+"  This is used for the first step of linking to find Object Pool mappings.  \n"
+// Their separator here is a bare newline, not a line padded to 76 like every
+// other one. Measured, not guessed -- the check compares trailing whitespace.
+"\n"
+"Shorebird options:                                                          \n"
+"--shorebird                                                                 \n"
+"  Produce Shorebird formatted JSON, requires --out.                         \n"
+// Shorebird lists six more flags at this point, in this order: --no_pp_hash,
+// --dump_object_pool_link_data, --dump_blobs, --dump_object_pool,
+// --dump_class_table and --disassemble. They are deliberately NOT listed here,
+// because this binary does not implement them, and a help text advertising a
+// flag the parser rejects is worse than one that is short.
+//
+// The omission is pinned rather than left to memory:
+// analyze_snapshot_help_check.sh takes their --help, deletes exactly those six
+// blocks, and requires what is left to equal ours byte for byte. Every line
+// above is therefore checked against theirs on every run, and what is missing
+// is a number that has to shrink deliberately.
+//
+// None of the six is small. --dump_object_pool_link_data and --no_pp_hash need
+// op_subgraph_hash, a private subsystem this tree does not have --
+// ObjectPoolLinkInfo::WriteToStream is already here and byte-verified against
+// their files, so what is missing is the producer, not the format.
+// --dump_blobs needs the four region sizes, which
+// Dart_SnapshotAnalyzerInformation does not carry.
 "If omitting [<vm-flags>] the VM parsing the snapshot is created with the    \n"
 "following default flags:                                                    \n"
 "--enable_mirrors=false                                                      \n"
-- 
